---
// The Methodology page: how prices are compared, how COAs and tiers work, and
// what "date checked" means. River approved the wording on 2026-11-05.
// Limits and comparison amounts come from src/settings.ts and categories/*.yaml,
// so this page updates itself when those change.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { categoryList } from '../categories';
import { settings } from '../settings';

// Extra words for categories where "what counts" isn't obvious from the comparison amount.
const counts: Record<string, string> = {
  ashwagandha: 'Extract or root powder, as labeled. The two aren’t the same thing at the same weight.',
  magnesium: 'The elemental magnesium on the Supplement Facts panel, never the compound weight.',
  'fish-oil': 'EPA plus DHA from the Supplement Facts panel, not the total amount of oil.',
  multivitamin: 'One daily serving as the label defines it: price ÷ servings in the container.',
  'vitamin-k2': 'Vitamin K2 only. Vitamin K1 isn’t counted.',
};
const rows = categoryList
  .filter((c) => c.published)
  .map((c) => ({ name: c.name, path: c.path, amount: c.referenceDose.label.replace(/^per /, ''), daily: c.daily?.label, note: counts[c.id] }));
import { ingredientRules, levelLabels } from '../ingredients/rules';
import { levelOrder } from '../lib/ingredients';
const rulesByLevel = levelOrder.map((level) => ({ level, rules: ingredientRules.filter((r) => r.level === level) })).filter((g) => g.rules.length);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How Sup Search collects prices, converts them to a cost per fixed amount of each ingredient, judges lab reports, assigns transparency tiers, and dates every value."
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">How we collect prices, compare them fairly, judge lab reports, and grade testing transparency.</p>

    <h2>How prices are collected</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A person opens a single public product page, such as the brand’s own store or a retailer like Vitacost, GNC,
        Target or Walmart, and copies the price shown that day. There’s no automated scraping.
      </li>
      <li>The price recorded is the regular price you’d pay that day, before shipping.</li>
      <li>
        Each price is saved with the store, the number of servings in that store’s container, the page link, and the
        date it was checked.
      </li>
      <li>Amounts per serving come from the product’s Supplement Facts panel, not the marketing text.</li>
      <li>Products whose values our editor hasn’t yet checked against their sources are marked “Not yet reviewed.”</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we compare prices</h2>
    <p>
      Every price is converted to the cost of one fixed amount of the main ingredient, so a big tub and a small bottle
      can be compared fairly:
    </p>
    <p class="formula">
      cost per comparison amount = price ÷ (servings in the container × amount per serving) × comparison amount
    </p>
    <div class="table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Compared per</th><th scope="col">What counts</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><a href={r.path}>{r.name}</a></th>
                <td>{r.amount}</td>
                <td>{r.note ?? 'The amount on the Supplement Facts panel.'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost per 30 days</strong> is shown only where a daily amount has been set:
        {rows.filter((r) => r.daily).map((r) => `${r.name} (${r.daily})`).join('; ')}.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Best price</strong> is the lowest cost per comparison amount among prices that aren’t stale. If every
        price is stale, the lowest stale one is shown and marked stale.
      </li>
      <li>
        A lower cost per amount doesn’t mean a product works better. Forms differ, and we record the form without
        adjusting the price for it or for absorption claims.
      </li>
      <li>We don’t earn commissions, and nothing but this formula decides the order.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How COAs are judged</h2>
    <p>
      A certificate of analysis (COA) is a lab report. For each one we record the link, the lab, whether the lab is
      ISO/IEC 17025 accredited (yes, no or unknown), the lot number, the test date, whether it tests the finished product
      or the raw material, and which tests it reports. Each category lists the tests that matter for it, such as heavy
      metals, microbes, or oxidation for fish oil.
    </p>
    <p>
      Certifications (NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, USP Verified, BSCG, Clean Label Project, and IFOS for fish
      oil) count only when they appear on the certifier’s own website, never because a brand says so.
    </p>

    <h2>Transparency tiers</h2>
    <p>The tier is shown next to the price and never merged into one score.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Tier A:</strong> a current third-party certification and a COA from an accredited lab on the finished
        product.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tier B:</strong> a COA exists, but accreditation, finished-product status, or date can’t be confirmed.
        A product with a current certification but no qualifying COA is also Tier B.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Tier C:</strong> no COA or certification found.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A certification is current if its listing was re-checked on the certifier’s site within the last
      {settings.certificationCurrentForMonths} months. A COA counts toward Tier A only if the lab is accredited, it tests
      the finished product, and its test date is less than {settings.coaStaleAfterMonths} months old. Each product page
      shows why it got its tier.
    </p>

    {/* Ingredient grades section: added by Claude on 2026-11-05 at River's request; not part of the wording River approved. */}
    <h2 id="ingredient-grade">Ingredient grades</h2>
    <p>
      Separate from the price and the tier, each product gets a grade for its added (non-active) ingredients: sweeteners,
      colors, sugars, fillers and the like. It is worked out in code from the ingredient list we recorded and the rules
      below, and it never changes the order of a comparison table.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A:</strong> none of the product’s added ingredients are on the list below.</li>
      <li><strong>B:</strong> contains at least one ingredient on the list below.</li>
      <li><strong>Not graded:</strong> we haven’t confirmed the product’s full ingredient list yet.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every flag gives the same grade. The headings below say what kind of source backs each one: a regulator that has acted, official advice to limit, or individual studies. A flag describes what its source says about the ingredient in general, not the
      amount in a particular product, so it isn’t a safety verdict on that product. An ingredient joins this list only
      with a source we’ve read: a regulator’s page or a published study.
    </p>
    {rulesByLevel.map((g) => (
      <>
        <h3>{levelLabels[g.level]}</h3>
        <ul>
          {g.rules.map((r) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{r.name}.</strong> {r.finding}{' '}
              {r.sources.map((src, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && ' '}
                  <a href={src.url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Source: {src.publisher}{src.published && `, ${src.published.getUTCFullYear()}`}</a>
                </>
              ))}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </>
    ))}

    <h2>What “date checked” means</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Price:</strong> the day the store’s page was read and showed that price. A price is marked stale after
        {settings.priceStaleAfterDays} days.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Certification:</strong> the day the certifier’s own listing was read. It stops counting as current after
        {settings.certificationCurrentForMonths} months.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>COA:</strong> the test date printed on the lab report, not the day we found it. A COA is marked stale
        after {settings.coaStaleAfterMonths} months.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Update schedule</h2>
    <p>
      Prices are re-checked by hand, oldest first. Anything past its limit is labeled stale on the site until it’s
      re-checked.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site doesn’t cover</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We don’t test products ourselves. We report what labels, stores, certifiers and published lab reports say.</li>
      <li>We don’t rank products by how well they work, and nothing here is medical advice.</li>
      <li>We don’t adjust prices for absorption or “bioavailability” claims.</li>
      <li>
        We cover products sold in the US whose label states how much of the main ingredient is in each serving. Products
        are left out when their label or price couldn’t be read.
      </li>
      <li>Prices don’t include shipping.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Affiliate disclosure</h2>
    <p>We don’t earn commissions from any link on this site.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 46rem;
    padding-block: 2rem 1rem;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .prose li + li {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .formula {
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--rule);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }
  .table {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
